<USER_REQUEST>
Calculate the change in entropy (ΔS) for the reaction in which 3 moles of carbon monoxide (CO) gas is burned with excess oxygen gas to form 3 moles of carbon dioxide (CO2) gas. The reaction is carried out at constant pressure and a temperature of 298 K.
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
To calculate the change in entropy (ΔS) for the reaction, we can use the following equation:

ΔS = ΔS° (products) - ΔS° (reactants)

where ΔS° is the standard entropy change for each substance.

The reaction is:

3CO (g) + 3O2 (g) → 3CO2 (g)

We need the standard entropies (ΔS°) of CO, O2, and CO2 at 298 K. These values can be found in standard thermodynamic tables:

ΔS°(CO) = 186.75 J/(mol·K)
ΔS°(O2) = 205.66 J/(mol·K)
ΔS°(CO2) = 233.07 J/(mol·K)

Now we can calculate the change in entropy:

ΔS = 3 * ΔS°(CO2) - (3 * ΔS°(CO) + 3 * ΔS°(O2))

ΔS = 3 * 233.07 J/(mol·K) - (3 * 186.75 J/(mol·K) + 3 * 205.66 J/(mol·K))

ΔS = 699.21 J/(mol·K) - (550.25 J/(mol·K) + 616.98 J/(mol·K))

ΔS = 699.21 J/(mol·K) - 1167.23 J/(mol·K)

ΔS = -468.02 J/(mol·K)

The change in entropy for the reaction is -468.02 J/(mol·K).
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

