","To determine the polarity of the carbon-oxygen bond in CO2, we use electronegativity values. Electroneeggty is the ability of an atom to attract electrons in a chemical bond. The electronegativity values for carbon (C) and oxygen (O) are:  - Electronegativity of carbon (C) = 0.54 - Electronegativity of oxygen (O) = 3.43 The difference in electronegativity (Δχ) between O and C is: Δχ = 3.43 - 0.54 = 2.89 A difference in electronegativity greater than 0.7 indicates a polar bond. Since 2.89 > 0.7, the carbon-oxygen bond in CO2 is polar. The oxygen atom has a greater electronegativity, so it will have a partial negative charge (δ-), and the carbon atom will have a partial positive charge (δ+). Thus, the bond is polar with a negative charge on the oxygen and a positive charge on the carbon.  $\boxed{\text{The carbon-oxygen bond in CO2 is polar.}}$",relevance,"# Task Description
