","a) **Series System:**  
In a series system, the system fails if and only if one of its components fails.  
- Let \( n \) be the number of components in the series system.  
- Let \( R_i(t) \) be the reliability of the \( i \)-th component at time \( t \), for \( i = 1, \ldots, n \).  
- The system reliability \( R_S(t) \) is the product of the individual component reliabilities:  
\[
R_S(t) = R_1(t) \cdot R_2(t) \cdot \cdots \cdot R_n(t) = \prod_{i=1}^{n} R_i(t).
\]  
This is because the system only survives if all components survive.  

b) **Parallel System:**  
In a parallel system, the system survives if and only if at least one component survives.  
- Let \( n \) be the number of components in the parallel system.  
- Let \( R_i(t) \) be the reliability of the \( i \)-th component at time \( t \), for \( i = 1, \ldots, n \).  
- The system reliability \( R_S(t) \) is the complement of the probability that all components fail:  
\[
R_S(t) = 1 - \prod_{i=1}^{n} (1 - R_i(t)).
\]  
This is because the system fails only if all components fail.  

c) **Combination of Series and Parallel Structures:**  
For a system with a combination of series and parallel structures, the reliability is calculated by breaking the system into its components and then combining them appropriately.  

