","(a) The magnitude of the acceleration of the proton is calculated using Newton's second law:  
\[ F = m a \]  
The force on the proton is given by the electric field:  
\[ F = q E \]  
Substituting \( F = q E \) into \( F = m a \), we get:  
\[ q E = m a \]  
Solving for \( a \):  
\[ a = \frac{q E}{m} = \frac{(1.60 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{C})(500 \, \text{N/C})}{1.67 \times 10^{-27} \, \text{kg}} = 4.8 \times 10^{8} \, \text{m/s}^2 \]  
The magnitude of the acceleration of the proton is \(\boxed{4.8 \times 10^8 \, \text{m/s}^2}\).  

(b) The time \( t \) it takes for the proton to reach a speed \( v = 35,000 \, \text{m/s} \) is calculated using the kinematic equation:  
\[ v = v_0 + a t \]  
Since the proton starts from rest, \( v_0 = 0 \). Thus:  
\[ v = a t \]  
