One mole of an ideal gas is contained under a weightless piston in a vertical cylinder at temperature \( T \). The space above the piston is open to the atmosphere. What work must be performed to isothermally expand the gas volume under the piston by a factor of \( n \) by slowly raising the piston? Assume negligible friction between the piston and the cylinder walls.<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","The work done by the gas during an isothermal expansion is given by:  
\[ W = \int_{V_i}^{V_f} P \, dV \]  
For an ideal gas, the pressure \( P \) is related to the volume \( V \) by the ideal gas law:  
\[ P = \frac{RT}{V} \]  
where \( R \) is the gas constant and \( T \) is the temperature.  

Given that the volume is expanded by a factor of \( n \), the final volume \( V_f \) is \( nV_i \), where \( V_i \) is the initial volume. Substituting \( P \) into the work integral:  
\[ W = \int_{V_i}^{nV_i} \frac{RT}{V} \, dV \]  
\[ W = RT \ln V \bigg|_{V_i}^{nV_i} \]  
\[ W = RT \ln (nV_i) - RT \ln (V_i) \]  
\[ W = RT \ln \left( \frac{nV_i}{V_i} \right) \]  
\[ W = RT \ln n \]  

Thus, the work done is \( \boxed{RT \ln n} \).",relevance,"# Task Description
