<start_of_turn>model
","To compute \(V_{BS}\), we use the threshold voltage equation for a p-channel MOSFET:

\[
V_T = V_{T0} - \frac{q \phi_s}{2 \phi_s} V_{BS}
\]

where:
- \(V_T\) is the threshold voltage,
- \(V_{T0}\) is the threshold voltage at \(V_{BS} = 0\),
- \(q\) is the electron charge (\(1.6 \times 10^{-19} \text{ C}\)),
- \(\phi_s\) is the surface work function,
- \(V_{BS}\) is the body-to-source voltage.

Given:
- The threshold voltage is shifted by \(-1.0\) V, so \(V_T = V_{T0} - 1.0\) V.
- The oxide thickness \(t_{ox} = 500 \times 10^{-10} \text{ cm}\),
- The donor doping concentration \(N_d = 10^{16} \text{ cm}^{-3}\).

First, we calculate \(\phi_s\) using the formula:

\[
\phi_s = \frac{q \sqrt{2 \epsilon_s t_{ox} N_d}}{2 \sqrt{2 \epsilon_s t_{ox}}}
\]

where \(\epsilon_s\) is the oxide permittivity (\(3.9 \times 10^{-14} \text{ F/cm}\)).

Substituting the values:

\[
\phi_s = \frac{1.6 \times 10^{-19} \times \sqrt{2 \times 3.9 \times 10^{-14} \times 500 \times 10^{-10} \times 10^{16}}}{2 \sqrt{2 \times 3.9 \times 10^{-14} \times 500 \times 10^{-10}}}
\]

\[
\phi_s = \frac{1.6 \times 10^{-19} \times \sqrt{1.9 \times 10^{-10}}}{2 \sqrt{1.9 \times 10^{-10}}}
\]

\[
\phi_s = \frac{1.6 \times 10^{-19} \times 10^{-5} \sqrt{1.9}}{2 \times 10^{-5} \sqrt{1.9}}
\]

\[
\phi_s = 0.8 \text{ V}
\]

Now, substitute \(\phi_s = 0.8\) V and \(V_T = V_{T0} - 1.0\) V into the threshold voltage equation:

\[
V_{T0} - 1.0 = V_{T0} - \frac{q \phi_s}{2 \phi_s} V_{BS}
\]

\[
-1.0 = -\frac{1.6 \times 10^{-19} \times 0.8}{2 \times 0.8} V_{BS}
\]

\[
-1.0 = -0.5 \times 10^{-19} \text{ V}
\]

\[
V_{BS} = \frac{-1.0}{0.5 \times 10^{-19}}
\]

\[
