The Coriolis effect is named after the German physicist C. Coriolis, although the Coriolis effect was first described by the French physicist Eövarte, who first described the Coriolis effect in 1999. The Coriolis effect is a pseudo force that acts on moving objects in a rotating frame of reference. In the case of ocean currents, the reference frame is the Earth's surface.

